\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Cost of proving $N = 2^{n}$ integer multiplications $x \cdot y = z$, $n = 15, \ldots  , 22$, for random $32$-bit integers $x, y$ (so that $z$ is a $64$-bit integer) with \cref  {c:iop_pimsat}: one R1CS constraint per multiplication over $\mathbb  {Z}$, projected to a prime $q$ sampled after the commitment from an interval $[2^{b-1}, 2^{b})$ with $b$ between $108$ and $111$ depending on the shape, a Spartan PIOP over $\ensuremath  {\mathbb  {F}}_q$ (with a $3$-variable univariate skip), bitification, and the {$\mathbb  {B}\text  {it}\mathbb  {Z}$}\ opening of the $128$ bits committed per multiplication ($\ell = 2^{n+7}$ bits in cells of $W = 1$ bit, i.e.\ $128$ cells per multiplication). Security profile $\lambda = 100$: every round-by-round error is at most $2^{-100.0}$, the binding term being \texttt  {step5\_3:ligerito-tracked}; the commitment is opened with ring switching and Ligerito~\cite  {ligerito} over a Reed--Solomon code of rate $1/2$ over $\ensuremath  {\mathbb  {F}}_{2^{128}}$ (initial folding of $2^{4}$ rows), configured for $100$ bits. Prover columns: \emph  {Commit} is bit packing plus the commitment; \emph  {PIOP} is the prime projection, the Spartan PIOP and the (microsecond-scale) bitification step; \emph  {grand products}, \emph  {ring switch} and \emph  {Ligerito} are the three parts of the {$\mathbb  {B}\text  {it}\mathbb  {Z}$}\ opening as in \cref  {tab:f2z-raw-performance}; \emph  {Total} is end to end and includes the commitment. Proof columns: \emph  {PIOP} is the Spartan messages and grinding nonces; \emph  {Non-Lig.} is the opening's integer folds, GKR, sumcheck and ring-switch messages; \emph  {Ligerito} is the Ligerito proof; KB $= 1000$ bytes. Apple M4 (10 cores: 4 performance + 6 efficiency), 16\,GB, 8 threads; medians of 5 runs after one warm-up; witness generation and the one-time relation preparation are excluded.}}{51}{table.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:f2z-u32-mul}{{8}{51}{Cost of proving $N = 2^{n}$ integer multiplications $x \cdot y = z$, $n = 15, \ldots , 22$, for random $32$-bit integers $x, y$ (so that $z$ is a $64$-bit integer) with \cref {c:iop_pimsat}: one R1CS constraint per multiplication over $\ZZ $, projected to a prime $q$ sampled after the commitment from an interval $[2^{b-1}, 2^{b})$ with $b$ between $108$ and $111$ depending on the shape, a Spartan PIOP over $\FF _q$ (with a $3$-variable univariate skip), bitification, and the \ftwoz \ opening of the $128$ bits committed per multiplication ($\codedim = 2^{n+7}$ bits in cells of $W = 1$ bit, i.e.\ $128$ cells per multiplication). Security profile $\lambda = 100$: every round-by-round error is at most $2^{-100.0}$, the binding term being \texttt {step5\_3:ligerito-tracked}; the commitment is opened with ring switching and Ligerito~\cite {ligerito} over a Reed--Solomon code of rate $1/2$ over $\FF _{2^{128}}$ (initial folding of $2^{4}$ rows), configured for $100$ bits. Prover columns: \emph {Commit} is bit packing plus the commitment; \emph {PIOP} is the prime projection, the Spartan PIOP and the (microsecond-scale) bitification step; \emph {grand products}, \emph {ring switch} and \emph {Ligerito} are the three parts of the \ftwoz \ opening as in \cref {tab:f2z-raw-performance}; \emph {Total} is end to end and includes the commitment. Proof columns: \emph {PIOP} is the Spartan messages and grinding nonces; \emph {Non-Lig.} is the opening's integer folds, GKR, sumcheck and ring-switch messages; \emph {Ligerito} is the Ligerito proof; KB $= 1000$ bytes. Apple M4 (10 cores: 4 performance + 6 efficiency), 16\,GB, 8 threads; medians of 5 runs after one warm-up; witness generation and the one-time relation preparation are excluded}{table.caption.50}{}}
